  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "ACE inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs) are both top-tier, first-line treatments for high blood pressure, but ARBs generally cause fewer side effects.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "ARBs generally cause fewer side effects"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both drug classes lower blood pressure by relaxing your blood vessels, but they block the body's chemical signals in slightly different ways."
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "ACE Inhibitors (e.g., Lisinopril, Enalapril)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "How they work: Stop your body from making a hormone that narrows blood vessels."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Pros: Highly effective, well-studied over many decades, and widely available as low-cost generics."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cons: Frequently cause a lingering, dry hacking cough in some people. Can rarely cause dangerous swelling of the face or throat (angioedema)."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "ARBs (e.g., Losartan, Valsartan)"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "How they work: Block the receptor sites where that same narrowing hormone tries to attach."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Pros: Just as effective as ACE inhibitors at lowering blood pressure, with a much lower risk of a dry cough or tissue swelling."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cons: Can also raise potassium levels or affect kidney function, similar to ACE inhibitors."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Which is better?"
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Medical guidelines from organizations like the American Heart Association list both classes as equal, top choices. However, large-scale safety studies show that patients taking ARBs are less likely to stop treatment due to annoying side effects. Many doctors now prefer starting with an ARB if cost and insurance coverage allow.",
        "snippet_links": [
          {
            "text": "American Heart Association",
            "link": "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/HYPERTENSIONAHA.120.16667"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Note: Neither drug should be taken during pregnancy, and you should never take an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together at the same time."
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you are deciding on a treatment plan, I can help you:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Compare specific drugs (e.g., side effects of lisinopril vs. losartan)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Review other health conditions (e.g., diabetes, kidney disease) that affect your choice"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Prepare questions for your doctor or pharmacist"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
